  "strategy": "### Approach and Rationale\n\nWe approach the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by combining structured initialization with adaptive optimization. The central idea is to leverage Lyapunov exponents to ensure strong theoretical guarantees while maintaining practical applicability. \n\n**Key Rationale:**\n1. **Bottleneck Analysis:** Current solutions inefficiently balance exploration and exploitation, leading to oscillation between local minima.\n2. **Central Idea:** Employ exponential Lyapunov tuning to dynamically adjust the optimization landscape, escaping plateaued regions via curvature-based restarts.\n3. **Limitations:** Requires careful NLP matrix maintenance; gradient computation complexity dominates at n\u2248600, but parallelization mitigation helps.\n\n### Mathematical Specification\n\n**Variables:**\n- `h(x) \u2208 [0,1]` continuous function\n- Grid size `n_points = 606`\n- Integral constraint: \u2211h_i = n_points/2\n\n**Objective:**\nMinimize C\u2085 = max_n \u2211_{i} h_i \u00b7 (1 - h_{i+n})/dx_param\u2083\u2084\n\n**Constraints:**\n1. 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n2. \u2211h_i = 503 (606/2 rounded)\n\n**Gradient Definition:**\n\u0394_n = (h_i - \u03bc\u2099) \u00b7 exp(\u03b2\u03b3sin\u00b2(h_i\u00b7\u03b4_i)) \n\n### Implementation Procedure\n\n#### 1. Representation and Validation\n```python\ndef validate_grid(n):\n    if n not in [600, 800, 1000]:\n        raise ValueError(f\"Unsupported grid size: {n}\")\n    if sum(1 if n % 2 != 0 else 0) % 2 != 0:\n        n += 1 - (n % 2)\n    return n\n```\n\n#### 2. Initialization\n```python\ndef init_entropy_grid(n, seed):\n    np.random.seed(seed)\n    # One-time exponential random initial distribution\n    h = np.random.exponential(scale=0.8, size=n)\n    h = (h < 1.5).astype(float)\n    h = project_feasible(h, n/2)\n    return h\n```\n\n#### 3. Optimization Loop\nUse grid size n=605 to minimize PAPR effects (Primes Under Penalty Readjustment)\n\n```python\ndef optimize_with_exp_decay(h_init, grid, cooling_factor=0.9995):\n    h = h_init\n    C = compute_c5(h)\n    cur_temp = 1.0\n    obj_history = []\n    \n    for i in range(150000 if len(grid)==605 else 100000):\n        if cur_temp < 1e-4:\n            break\n        new_h = local_search_exp(h, grid)\n        new_C = compute_c5(new_h)\n        delta = new_C - C.verbose(obj_history)\n        if delta < 0 or np.random.rand() < np.exp(-delta / cur_temp):\n            h, C = new_h, new_C\n        cur_temp *= cooling_factor\n        i += 1\n    return h, C\n```\n\n### Search Choices & Variations\nDefault variant: Exponential Grid Contraction\nAlternate variants switch between:\n1. Grid sizes [605, 700, 800]\n2. Local search strength factor \u03b1\u2208(0.6, 0.9)\n3. Adaptive threshold for delta acceptance\n\n### Computational Budget\n- Cool time: 10.6 seconds with matrix verification\n- I/O pipelines utilize expression trees\n- Early exit on 50ms improvement plateau\n\n### Validation & Return Contract\n```python\ndef post_processing(h, n):\n    # One-time curvature smoothing\n    h_smoothed = np.interp(\n        np.linspace(0, 1, n),\n        np.linspace(0, 1, len(h)),\n        h\n    )\n    integral = np.add.reduce(h_smoothed)/400\n    deviation = abs(integral - n/2)/n/2\n    score = compute_c5(h_smoothed)\n    return np.clip(h_smoothed, 0, 1), score, round(integral)\n```\n\n### Output Contract\nReturns h_values, c5_bound, n_points\nEnsures:\n1. 0 \u2264 h_values[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n2. abs(sum(h_values) - n_points/2) < 1\n3. c5_bound < 0.388",
